      en: "Chau Say Tevoda is a Hindu temple built in the mid-12th century under King Suryavarman II, located east of Angkor Thom. Dedicated to Vishnu, it features a central sanctuary and four satellite towers on a laterite platform. The sandstone carvings are exceptionally detailed, with intricate lintels depicting scenes from the Ramayana and Mahabharata. Unlike its counterpart Thommanon, Chau Say Tevoda shows unfinished sections, suggesting construction halted abruptly. The temple's layout is cruciform, with gopuras at each cardinal point. It was restored in the 1960s using anastylosis techniques.",
