      en: "Prasat Kravan is a 10th-century Hindu temple built in 921 AD by noble courtiers, not a monarch. Located south of Angkor Wat, it consists of five brick towers on a single laterite platform. The interior features unique brick bas-reliefs of Vishnu with eight arms and his avatars, a rare medium in Khmer art. The central tower originally housed a statue of Vishnu. The temple was restored by the French in the 1930s. Its name means 'Avocado Temple' due to the avocado trees in the area. The towers taper upward, with lotus-shaped crowns.",
